+# Source normalization: Kourovka 21.16 (ID 2525)
+
+The October 2026 maintained 21st edition, printed/PDF page 179, asks whether a group can have finite width as a group and fail finite width as a monoid. Its definition quantifies over **every applicable generating set**, allowing the bound to depend on that set. Group words permit inverses; monoid words do not. We include the empty word for the identity, which has no effect on finiteness. No topology, measurability or cardinality assumption is imposed. The target has no solution marker and no entry in the October updates-only file.
+
+Write CB for finite group width and MB for finite monoid width. Avoid the ambiguous unqualified phrase 'Bergman property': different authors use it for CB or for CB together with uncountable cofinality. MB implies CB by applying MB to the symmetric set S union S^{-1}. Bergman's 2006 paper, Section 3 before Question 9, proves CB plus uncountable cofinality implies MB. Here uncountable cofinality means there is no increasing countable exhaustion by proper subgroups (including the usual finitely generated convention). Thus a separating example must have countable cofinality. An arbitrary unbounded symmetric subadditive length refutes the stronger conjunction, and does not by itself refute CB.
+
+Bergman Question 9 also asks whether any CB group of countable cofinality satisfies MB. The Kourovka target asks only for separation. Do not substitute this second question for the target. Nor does a proper positive submonoid which group-generates G answer the target: it fails the required positive generation of all G.
+
+Primary literature read:
+- G. M. Bergman, *Generating infinite symmetric groups*, BLMS 38 (2006), 429–440, DOI 10.1112/S0024609305018308. The bound source file is the author's arXiv version, with different page numbering; definitions (3),(4), Question9 and the proof preceding it are on pp4–5. Lemma11 on p6 supplies a warning about positive cones, not a separating example.
+- Maltcev's thesis, Chapter2 pp21–28, incorporates work with Mitchell and Ruškuc, *The Bergman property for semigroups*, JLMS80 (2009),212–232, DOI10.1112/jlms/jdp025. It explicitly states the group-to-semigroup converse is unknown. Its later brief assertion about Khelif's example is not treated as an independently verified construction here.
+- A. Khelif, *À propos de la propriété de Bergman*, CRMath342 (2006),377–380, DOI10.1016/j.crma.2006.01.010. This is an announcement with brief indications and says proofs will appear later. Counterexample3 asserts a CB group with an index-two subgroup not CB, hence the group is not strongly bounded. We do not reconstruct or certify its missing HNN construction. Theorems about solvable-by-finite groups and other classes are credited reports, not dependencies of our proofs.
+- Jarnevic, Osin and Oyakawa, *Generic length functions on countable groups*, JAlgebra2023, arXiv2206.10712, Corollary1.6: countable mixed-identity-free groups have an unbounded Cayley graph. The introduction still states the unrestricted infinite-countable-group question as open. This narrower historical statement does not establish a current exhaustive literature verdict.
+
+The exact October source and targeted current searches gave no verified resolution. These are bounded source checks, not a novelty certificate. All raw sources and imported records remain outside the public packet; SOURCE_MANIFEST records reproducible URLs and hashes.
