+# Full independent review: Kourovka 21.16
+
+PASS for the five scoped reductions, retaining the additive turn-3 control clarification. Original abstract CB-versus-MB separation remains unsolved, five of five author turns. No infinite separating example or universal implication is established.
+
+Author final manifest SHA-256: 1e13e0fd20d6a43f6f3a77ba9e7182cd3777b61786f615000e2ec3fd12627490. Final WIP: e8260c51082d94598a72a3e54034a577ddc50ba3. Reviewed all five complete proofs, source normalization, the clarification and full replay. No further mandatory mathematical correction is required.
+
+## Exact source and credited scope
+
+Visually checked October 2026 Kourovka printed page 179, Problem 21.16. The bound may depend on the generating set. Group words allow inverses; monoid words must positively generate all elements. No regularity or topology is present. Checked Bergman's definitions (3),(4), the full proof before Question 9, and Question 9 itself. The conjunction of uncountable cofinality and Cayley boundedness implies monoid boundedness by that credited proof. Neither the stronger conjunction nor a bound for just one symmetric generating set can replace CB.
+
+Khelif's announced finite-index example is credited as an announcement and is not a reconstructed proof dependency. It is not presented as the requested separation. The later countable-group paper and thesis references retain their historical scope rather than establishing a new exhaustive status claim. Rosendal's full compact topologically 2-Bergman proof, Proposition 3.9 in the retrieved author version, was checked. The packet's Baire argument is a scoped regularity result, not a conclusion for arbitrary abstract generating sets.
+
+## Turn 1
+
+For a genuinely monoid-generating S, each inverse has finite cost but its supremum may be infinite. Replacing each inverse letter in a bounded symmetric word gives exactly the inverse-cost criterion. Uniformly bounded orders suffice; a common exponent identity is unnecessary. The canonical symmetric cores exhaust G, and CB is applied to a core only when it generates all G. No individual subgroup is assumed CB. A finite supplement lies in a later core because finitely many elements and inverses have a common cost bound. Thus every subgroup in a separating exhaustion has no finite supplement and has infinite index. The integer example has finite diameter for its special symmetric set while failing CB for another set, correctly exposing the quantifier.
+
+## Turn 2
+
+The directed right-coset graph and positive shortest representatives are valid even for a nonnormal subgroup. The representative inverse costs form a finite maximum; they are not spuriously bounded by the number of cosets. Positive Schreier factors lie in H and telescope without inverse factors, proving actual monoid generation of H. This establishes ascent of MB to finite-index overgroups, without incorrectly asserting arbitrary descent of CB.
+
+Quotient preservation uses lifts and all kernel elements. For a strongly bounded kernel, the symmetric intersections of ambient positive balls exhaust the kernel; uncountable cofinality and CB bound the ambient length directly. The argument never assumes S intersect N generates N. Once that restriction is bounded, lifting a shortest quotient word gives the exact additive length comparison. General MB kernels lack this ambient-length bound, and the packet correctly leaves arbitrary extensions open.
+
+## Turn 3
+
+The finite-extension normal form h r_q emits at most one saturated generator per input letter. Factor-set constants and conjugate copies are both included. Their positive costs are uniformly bounded because there are finitely many representatives and each inverse has a positive expression. In the split case their inverse representatives are single quotient letters, giving the stated factor-three estimate. An invariant S gives equality on H by moving quotient letters without changing its letters' membership.
+
+Both directions of the CB_Q and MB_Q equivalences are justified. For arbitrary generators upstairs, directed Schreier rewriting gives a genuinely generating set downstairs, and finite-action saturation has a uniform ambient cost. The infinite-dihedral example has actual positive generation, exact diameter three for W, and still fails CB. It refutes preservation of that witness, not the original group assertion. The additive clarification accurately distinguishes integer normal-form tests from a finite cyclic scan; all frozen bytes remain preserved.
+
+## Turn 4
+
+Finite normal generation first supplies a symmetric conjugacy generating set, to which CB gives bounded normal width. Conjugation invariance of the particular positive set then bounds every such conjugate's cost. The resulting conclusion is restricted to those witnesses, not all positive sets. The weaker uniform conjugation-cost condition is sufficient for the same reason.
+
+The inverse-producing relation requires a fixed finite conjugator family and a uniform number of positive letters. Positive generation bounds that family's costs, and substitution gives the advertised inverse bound. Generator-dependent unbounded conjugator costs would make the argument circular; they are explicitly excluded. No unproved finite normal generation theorem for arbitrary CB groups is used.
+
+## Turn 5
+
+The Cartesian formula allows infinite length. A bounded common coordinate length gives a product word by choosing and padding coordinate words. Unbounded finite factor diameters permit a countable diagonal selection with infinite product length, so the Cartesian set fails algebraic generation rather than yielding a finite-valued unbounded metric. Direct sums fail CB by their different symmetric support metric.
+
+The closed inverse-limit formula uses nested closed conditions in the compact Cartesian space of m-tuples from S. Surjective bonding maps and projection equality ensure nonempty finite-level conditions; compatibility identifies the limit product. The 2-adic example is only densely generated, not algebraically generated as either a monoid or a group by its specified element.
+
+The Baire proof correctly uses a positive product, not a difference set. If B_n is comeagre in nonempty U, then for x in UU the open set U intersect xU^-1 is nonempty, and B_n and xB_n^-1 are both comeagre there. A point in their intersection gives x=ab with a,b in B_n. Compactness then reduces covering by translates of UU to finitely many translating elements with finite positive costs. This proves boundedness under the stated word-ball Baire condition. Sigma-compact sets in compact Hausdorff groups and analytic sets in compact Polish groups satisfy that condition; mere Baire regularity of S alone is correctly not assumed closed under multiplication images. The original imposes none of these hypotheses.
+
+## Reproducibility and final boundary
+
+Full author replay passed 311,547 exact controls, 156 bindings and seven primary PDF hashes. Independent standard-library controls passed 6,833 assertions without author imports: directed and symmetric distances in finite dihedral groups, arbitrary positive generating subsets, positive Schreier generators and costs, and the Cartesian maximum-distance identity. These finite tests do not certify CB or MB of an infinite group, prove Baire regularity, or decide the source problem.
+
+The remaining requirement is a genuinely monoid-generating, unbounded finite-valued positive metric on a group for which every symmetric generating set is bounded, or a universal proof that none exists. The five routes do not supply either. Approved original disposition is unsolved, five of five. Publish only frozen author and review whitelists, with a current-status wrapper and the target queue status/turn cells; no raw sources, unrelated edits or sixth search.
